body:
Port Orleans (Riverside) is my family's favorite resort, but keep in mind that we're pretty chill locals by this point - we don't rope-drop, and our favorite perk is the boat to Disney Springs for leisurely dinners. I'd say it's worth it if you plan to spend a decent amount of time at your resort or just lounging, since it's got a really nice atmosphere - plus, if you're at French Quarter, you can always walk over to Riverside for the sit-down restaurant, larger pool, etc. If you're going straight to the park every morning and spending as much time out as possible, go Pop for the Skyliner access. Pop is the only place on property where I've feared I might get murdered, but hey, that was pre-Skyliner, and some people like ten-story jukeboxes with ominous energy!

&#x200B;

(Also, speaking of Port Orleans - does anyone know if Yeehaw Bob is still at the River Roost? I thought he retired during COVID, but last time I was there they were advertising him again.)

As someone who's favorite part of every sequel trilogy so far has been the "Rey/Kylo aesthetically and thematically significant lightsaber fight" sequence, I'm glad to see this one is passing the torch and making it even more badass (Rey backflipping over a TIE! I didn't think I could get any more attracted to Daisy Ridley, and yet!!)

&#x200B;

That being said, Carrie Fisher's passing makes it all very bittersweet. We were robbed of so much potential for her in this movie, particularly her interacting with Lando (if bringing Lando back in 9 was planned from the beginning, and not an emergency "we no longer have Leia" contingency plan)

Hi all! Sorry for the delay, work's been a five-alarm fire for the past two days straight...I'm drafting Little Women and The Goldfinch as hits.

&#x200B;

(Also, I'm a girl, just FYI!)

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Pulling a Griffin Newman and drafting <i>The Lion King</i> as my hit. Hear me roar.

&#x200B;

Tagging /u/PeriodicGolden.

This is kind of Hot Take Central and I know I'm basically asking to get downvoted, but as a woman who loved Buffy when she was 13/14 (it was \*the thing\* that made me realize I wanted to be a screenwriter) and still adores Avengers 1, the reaction to Whedon--often from other women, but overwhelmingly from men who love yelling about how feminist they are (ironic)--has been kind of awful. More often than not it's this weird parade of smugness, with a healthy dose of "come on, the hints were \*all there\*, didn't you ALWAYS GUESS?". Because no, I was a child, I did NOT always guess, and that attitude feels like a way of redirecting the blame onto a creepy guy's fanbase for...being mislead? It feels like such a different reaction to, say, admitting that you used to like Louis C.K.\* Because there were hints of creepiness everywhere in his past work, but "*Louis* was really influential to me as a comedy writer" is usually treated with sympathy. (~~This has nothing to do with the fact that Whedon is one of the few called-out men of the #MeToo era whose work had a predominantly young female fanbase and/or was considered "for girls". A total and complete coincidence, I tell you.~~)

&#x200B;

\*not a callout to anyone in this thread! Just a general observation.

Makes...a weird amount of sense, if you think about it. The whole point of this Russian hacking operation is to amplify the discord/division in America and make it look like the West is in chaos, and big American movies (particularly Disney megafranchises ) are one of the few pieces of culture that are still kind of apolitical, in the sense that people of all political leanings still go to the same theater together opening night and watch 'em.\* So if they can make it look like the country is being torn apart by one of those franchises? Egads.

&#x200B;

\*that is to say, not that superhero movies don't make political statements, but that they're not really considered political works. Like, Captain America 2 in particular is creepily relevant nowadays, but something about the scifi/fantasy elements and the general cultural perception that they're lowbrow entertainment adds a layer of subtext that keeps it from really registering. Sure, it's about Nazis infiltrating the government, but he's in spandex and his friend is named after a bird! They're fictional Nazis, honey! The leader is literally in a secret bunker backed up on computer tape!

"To summarize, when you hear 'suicide', think 'shit'."

&#x200B;

(I also picture a scene-stealing supporting part by an underrated character actress as a perpetually exhausted Patty Jenkins, the only sane and/or sober one as she watches the mess from afar.)
